  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1212 AM CST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nDeparting upper trough will bring another day of cooler and \ncloudy conditions, with on and off lake-effect potential. Quiet \nweather will follow this weekend into next week.\n\nSnow across northern Wisconsin...Little change in the forecast \nacross north- central WI, as this setup has remained fairly \nsimilar. Typical cooler air over warmer water is generating lake- \neffect snow bands which occasionally stretch into northern \nWisconsin. In a lull at the moment, but expect additional develop \ntonight as lift in the low-levels increase. Once again \naccumulations will remain limited to far north-central WI (Vilas \nCounty) where another inch or so (20-50% chance for 1\" or more) \ncould accumulate tonight. Potential for more than 2\" is around \n10-20%. After ~14Z the lift is lost, and CAMs also feature less \nsnow getting into northern WI, so expect a steady downward trend \nthroughout Friday afternoon, with any remaining snow showers or \nflakes winding down by Friday evening.\n\nSnow across Door/Kewaunee counties...Questions regarding whether \na lake-induced precip band make it onshore tonight remain. A band \nstarted to develop a little after 00Z, which appeared to be mostly\nrain reaching the ground. If there were any snowflakes, they \nmelted upon contact with the ground. This band has since \ndissipated with more unorganized development now taking place over\ncentral Lake Michigan. Soundings still show favorable conditions \nin general for effective snow production tonight, with some low \nlevel instability and lift. The HRRR continues to be the most \naggressive with painting a narrow, heavier snow band of 4+ inches \nin parts of Door and Kewaunee counties near Lake Michigan. Other \nguidance is far less enthused. Probabilities for at least 1\" are \nas high as 60%, and as high as 20% for 4\" in a very localized \narea. A negating factor is the surface and ground/road \ntemperatures, which are expected to be just above freezing all \nnight. If an intense band makes it onshore this could overcome the\ntemperatures, but otherwise expect lighter snow would mostly melt\nwith accumulations limited to cold and grassy surfaces. Will be \nmonitoring the lake precip overnight and make forecast adjustments\nas necessary. \n\nElsewhere...Some flurries could occur across northern or eastern \nWisconsin tonight, otherwise dry weather is expected.\n\nRest of the forecast...Upper ridging and surface high pressure \nstart to take over by Sunday, leading to sunnier skies and a \ngradual warming trend with highs mostly in the 50s next week. The \nnext opportunity for rain could arrive Thu or Fri as we transition\nto southwest flow, but a lot of inconsistency that far out.",
